121 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 52 0 R
/E (Smaller importers who utilize 3PL providers \(e.g., FedEx, UPS, or DHL\) face significant hurdles, as the 3PL is typically the "importer of record." These entities lack direct legal recourse against the U.S. Government; therefore, any recovery is a commercial matter, not a regulatory one.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
122 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 52 0 R
/E (Management should evaluate potential recoveries based on enforceable contracts rather than "commercial negotiations." While public statements, such as the FedEx Statement of Intent, express a desire to pass through refunds to shippers, FedEx explicitly cautioned that the process "will depend in part on future guidance from the government." This dependency on external, future regulatory actions acts as a significant accounting trigger that typically precludes recognition. Unless an existing contract specifically provides for the rebate of invalidated tariffs, the "probable" threshold cannot be met, and the recovery must be treated as a contingent asset.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
